WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.320
Dying onscreen poses a unique challenge for
an actor, but some are more accustomed to

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.510
it than others. In fact, some stars seem like
they die during just about every movie they're

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:16.930
in. Grab a shovel and join us for a look at
a few of Hollywood's finest fake dead people.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:21.119
Spoilers ahead! Also, some not-very-pleasant-looking
movie massacres.

00:00:21.119 --> 00:00:22.880
Samuel L. Jackson

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:27.660
For a guy who's made an excellent living playing
imposing characters, Samuel L. Jackson seems

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.270
to meet his maker in the movies on a surprisingly
regular basis. This has more than a little

00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:37.219
to do with the law of averages. Simply put,
when you make over 100 films, you're bound

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:41.460
to be called upon for your share of death
scenes. But Jackson's many movie deaths—over

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:46.559
20 in all, and still growing—include some
of modern cinema's most memorable character

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:54.269
exits, like in Goodfellas, Jurassic Park,
and True Romance. But our favorites? There's

00:00:54.269 --> 00:00:58.789
the time he was suddenly slaughtered by a
shark in Deep Blue Sea:

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:00.309
"Aaahhhhh!"

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:07.000
And sent plummeting to his death by evil Emperor
Palpatine in Revenge of the Sith.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.240
"Aaaahhh!"

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.659
Gary Busey

00:01:11.659 --> 00:01:16.729
Character actor and real-life character Gary
Busey has died onscreen more than 20 times,

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:22.890
and we're pretty sure he's the only actor
to be killed for the cameras by Dolly Parton,

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:29.540
Ice-T, and Snoop Dogg. Other memorable offings
have come courtesy of his roles in Lethal

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:34.490
Weapon, Predator 2, and Point Break.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:36.189
Lance Henriksen

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:39.969
One of the most prolific murder victims in
movie history, Lance Henriksen has had the

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:47.350
honor of being offed by some pretty heavy
hitters. He died in The Terminator, Aliens,

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:55.750
and Alien vs. Predator, as well as one of
the many Hellraiser sequels. It's hard to

00:01:55.750 --> 00:02:00.329
pick a favorite from Henriksen's list of more
than 30 deaths, but ours might be the barely

00:02:00.329 --> 00:02:04.759
remembered Jean-Claude Van Damme movie Hard
Target, in which Henriksen's character has

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:08.610
a grenade shoved down his pants.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:08.960
John Hurt

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.080
Obviously, plenty of esteemed actors have
bit the big one in multiple movies, but John

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:18.660
Hurt takes cinematic corpsehood to another
level. Not only has he compiled more screen

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:24.350
deaths than arguably any other actor—over
40 in all, including Contact, Hellboy, and

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:29.040
V for Vendetta—but the Oscar-nominated legend
also boasts one of the most memorable exits

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:34.350
of all-time: he was the first victim of a
"chestburster" in Alien, suffering a gory

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:38.430
demise that's made millions of moviegoers
jump screaming out of their seats:

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.310
(screams)

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:42.900
Sean Bean

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:47.840
Sean Bean's tendency for playing doomed characters
is so well-known that it's inspired its own

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:52.250
website, countless memes, and a number of
in-depth analytical studies to determine whether

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:57.620
he has, in fact, died onscreen more than any
other actor. We'll leave that to the number-crunchers.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:03.640
Suffice it to say we've watched Bean pass
away more times than we, or he, can count,

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:09.540
like in Goldeneye, Patriot Games, and, of
course, Game of Thrones.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:11.060
He said to the Daily Mail:

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:17.790
"Maybe it's the quality of my death people
are fascinated by. I liked Lord of the Rings."

00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:19.290
Nicolas Cage

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.980
In real life, we suspect Nicolas Cage might
be immortal, but at the movies, he's uncommonly

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:28.510
susceptible to memorably messy deaths. He
took a stake through the chest in Vampire's

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:34.400
Kiss, drank himself to death in Leaving Las
Vegas, was burned to a crisp in Kick-Ass,

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.400
just to name a few. But no matter how many
times he dies onscreen, our favorite will

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.660
always be the insane way he perishes in the
all-around crazy 2006 remake of The Wicker

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.980
Man. Before that movie came along, we'd never
have guessed we needed to see Cage punch a

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:52.380
woman while wearing a bear suit, or watch
him howl in over-the-top agony while having

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:54.260
his face covered with bees:

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:56.300
"Aaahhhh! Aaahhh!"

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:59.860
Now that we've seen it, our lives will never
be the same.

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:00.980
Johnny Depp

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:05.370
Johnny Depp's distinguished career includes
over a dozen "killer" moments, including his

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:11.280
electrocution at the hands of Charlize Theron
in The Astronaut's Wife, and being eaten by

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.150
the Kraken in Pirates of the Caribbean: Dead
Man's Chest. His best movie death, however,

00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:20.849
will probably always be his first: in the
original Nightmare on Elm Street, Depp's character

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:25.629
is yoinked down into his bed after falling
asleep, sending a gushing fountain blood spraying

00:04:25.629 --> 00:04:29.659
up into the room after he's gone.

00:04:29.659 --> 00:04:34.139
Even in a franchise known for its spectacular
death scenes, it remains a standout.

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:35.020
Michael Biehn

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.469
After awhile, it got to the point where as
soon as you saw Michael Biehn's face on screen,

00:04:39.469 --> 00:04:43.629
you started counting down the minutes until
his inevitable death. He's arguably the most

00:04:43.629 --> 00:04:51.719
important victim in The Terminator, he's imploded
in The Abyss, and he's shot in the forehead

00:04:51.719 --> 00:04:57.960
by Val Kilmer in Tombstone…and those are
just the R.I.P. highlights from his first

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:02.770
10 years in the movies. He's gone on to the
great beyond more than 20 times, but our favorite

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:07.020
might be the crowbar to the head he took in
Stiletto, a little-seen 2008 effort featuring

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:12.319
Castle star Stana Katic.

00:05:12.319 --> 00:05:13.030
Danny Trejo

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:17.719
Danny Trejo seems like one of the few Hollywood
stars who could legitimately handle himself

00:05:17.719 --> 00:05:21.879
in a violent life-or-death situation, but
he's been on the losing end in more than a

00:05:21.879 --> 00:05:26.770
few mortal movie conflicts during his career.
Early on, he was often called on as cannon

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.900
fodder in action films, dying at the hands
of Charles Bronson in Death Wish IV and being

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.939
blown up in the Gary Busey flick Bulletproof,
but he's graduated to more exotic deaths in

00:05:36.939 --> 00:05:42.180
more recent years. Some of Trejo's more memorable
exits include his grisly demise in Predators.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.020
But for us, his best death scene will always
be the poignant mercy killing courtesy of

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.580
Robert De Niro in Michael Mann's classic 1995
action thriller Heat.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:52.770
Bruce Willis

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:57.710
Never mind Die Hard. For much of his career,
Bruce Willis' mantra seems to have been "die

00:05:57.710 --> 00:06:02.189
often." His dozen-plus on-screen deaths have
included crucial turning points in movies

00:06:02.189 --> 00:06:10.020
like Twelve Monkeys and The Jackal, and it
was his character who had the honor of saving

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.439
the planet by being blown up on an asteroid
in Armageddon. He's also had a couple of really

00:06:14.439 --> 00:06:18.949
stylish deaths courtesy of Robert Rodriguez
and Quentin Tarantino, biting the dust in

00:06:18.949 --> 00:06:24.669
Sin City and Grindhouse. His greatest death
might be his most mind-blowing: namely, the

00:06:24.669 --> 00:06:27.349
sneaky way he bit the dust in The Sixth Sense:

00:06:27.349 --> 00:06:29.430
"I see dead people"

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:35.650
But when he dies as a result of a time paradox
in the 2012 sci-fi classic, Looper? That one

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:36.849
might be our favorite.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:41.349
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:06:41.349 --> 00:06:45.659
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know which other actors should've made

00:06:45.659 --> 00:06:46.159
the list...

